  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="streams-title">The same shop can run five kinds of sale</h2>
      <p>An IT dealer often sells new goods, used goods, repairs and licences under one name. Each leaves different records, and mixing them is where the books usually go wrong. The points below are our general analysis.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Types of IT trade and their records, scrolls sideways on small screens">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">What you sell</th><th scope="col">What to keep</th><th scope="col">What to raise</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">New hardware</th><td>Purchase bills, and stock by serial number for costlier items</td><td>How distributor schemes and price-drop credit notes reach your books</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Warranty returns</th><td>A note of what went to the service centre, and what came back</td><td>How a replacement unit is recorded, so stock does not appear to grow or vanish</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Used and refurbished goods</th><td>Who you bought from, what you paid, and what you changed</td><td>Whether margin-based GST applies (below)</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Repairs</th><td>Job cards that split labour from parts</td><td>How a bill with both labour and parts should be treated</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Software licences</th><td>Publisher or distributor invoices, and licence details by customer</td><td>Whether you resell or only earn a commission, as the two are recorded differently</td></tr>
