<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="used-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="used-title">Used hardware has its own GST rule</h2>
      <p>Rule 32(5) of the CGST Rules covers a person dealing in buying and selling second-hand goods, used as such or after minor processing that does not change their nature. Where no input tax credit was taken on the purchase, the value of supply is the difference between the selling and purchase prices. A negative difference is ignored.</p>
      <p class="para">So your purchase record matters as much as your sale. Without it, you cannot show the margin. Ask whether your refurbishing counts as minor processing, and whether the rule suits you at all.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Goods that move between shops</h2>
      <p>Dealers in a dense market often borrow stock from a neighbour to complete an order. Each such movement should appear somewhere, or stock will not match by year end.</p>
      <p class="para">Rule 138 asks a registered person to furnish e-way bill details before moving a consignment worth more than ₹50,000, including for reasons other than supply. Ask how this applies to your deliveries and transfers, and what exceptions may apply.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="office-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="office-title">If you are an office upstairs</h2>
      <p>Nehru Place is one of the district centres the Delhi Development Authority developed in the 1970s, and firms still keep offices here. For a consultancy or IT services firm, stock rules are irrelevant. The work is invoices, client TDS, rent and staff.</p>
      <p class="para">See {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-consultants/", "accounting for consultants" %} and the {% routeLink "/locations/commercial-professional/", "commercial and professional areas" %} page.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Cash and walk-in sales</h2>
      <p>A 2012 Business Standard report described the market as doing much of its business in cash. That is dated, and UPI has likely changed it, so check your own mix. Either way, daily collections should agree with your sales, whichever way they were paid. See {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-traders/", "accounting for traders" %}.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="ask-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="ask-title">What to ask an accountant</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Have you handled IT resellers who also repair and sell used goods?</li>
        <li>How will you keep serial-numbered stock from drifting away from the books?</li>
        <li>How do you treat distributor credit notes that arrive months later?</li>
        <li>Can you work from our billing software, and which ones do you know?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>What we can say about this area</h2>
      <p>AccountOrbit has no office in Nehru Place and does not publish who is available here. This page explains the area's accounting, and being named here does not mean a professional is based nearby. Much of this work can also be done remotely if your records are digital. See {% routeLink "/locations/", "whether your accountant needs to be nearby" %}.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

